FINDINGS:
1. [MAJOR] tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:389 — the allow-list's accept branch lets the reviewer declare `read: "allow"` (or `glob`/`grep`/`list`), and the phase's own documentation calls that value a *widening*: tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:112-120 ("declaring `read: \"allow\"` would be an *widening* … an agent-level `read: \"allow\"` is appended after them, so it would win the last-match and re-allow them") and phases/phase-084/REPORT.md:314 ("`read` is deliberately **not** declared … an agent-level `read: \"allow\"` would be appended after them and re-allow them"). The enforcement contradicts the stated contract: `check_no_grants_beyond` returns `Ok` for `{"edit":"deny",…,"read":"allow"}` — nothing else in the suite forbids it — and the accept branch has no test on either side (the only test that touches a `REVIEWER_ALLOWED` key, `edge_a_narrow_pattern_allow_is_rejected` at :1151, returns through `permission_action`'s error before reaching :389). Consequence: a future edit that adds `read: "allow"` to the shipped `opencode.json` silently re-allows `.env` reads and the whole suite stays green. Fix: reject `read`/`glob`/`grep`/`list` on the reviewer outright (or drop `read` from `REVIEWER_ALLOWED`), and add a test asserting `agent.reviewer.permission` declares exactly the eleven denied keys.
2. [MAJOR] tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:508 — the round-9 generalisation stopped one level short: the top level is now exhaustively allow-listed (`TOP_LEVEL_KEYS` at :175, enforced by the loop at :486), but the per-agent checks are limited to `mode` and `permission` (:508-514) and `check_agent_set` (:450) checks names only, so every other key of `agent.reviewer` / `agent.verifier` is accepted silently. That is exactly the reach the round-9 change set out to close one level up — REPORT.md:26 names `instructions` as "the sharp one … a line there is a line the reviewer reads as an instruction from the pipeline rather than from its own contract" — and an agent-level prompt/instructions/tools key reaches the reviewer just as directly as the top-level one. What would settle it: dump the published schema's `AgentConfig` properties (the phase already validated against <https://opencode.ai/config.json> — FINDINGS.md:506-514) and check for a prompt/instructions field; if one exists, add an agent-level key allow-list plus a test that a `agent.reviewer.prompt` (or any unlisted agent key) is refused.
3. [MINOR] tests/agent_permissions_test.rs:226 — `git_run` captures stdout only and drops stderr, and `git` at :236 puts that empty stdout in the message, so every git failure reports "failed with status N: " with no reason. The report's own paste shows it: REPORT.md:373 ("`git ls-files --error-unmatch -- opencode.json` failed with status 1: ."). The sibling file already does it correctly — tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:301-303 includes `out.stderr`. Fix: capture stderr in `git_run` and put it in the error text.
4. [MINOR] phases/phase-084/REPORT.md:420 — "The twelve are exactly the tests that read the shipped file, because a document with a top-level key this file does not own is refused by the checker they all share" is false. Twenty tests read the shipped file (nineteen call `shipped_config()` — e.g. :600, :629, :659, :682, :702, :730, :758, :778, :855, :905, :924, :1033, :1052, :1121, :1152, :1183, :1200, :1381, :1413 — plus `project_config_ships_at_the_repository_root` at :544 via `check_file`), and only twelve failed: the eight that survived do not route through `check_document` (`project_config_denies_every_widening_permission_for_the_reviewer` calls `check_reviewer_denies` directly, `project_config_declares_primary_mode_for_both_agents` calls `check_mode`, the rest read the JSON directly) or short-circuit earlier (`edge_default_agent_key_is_rejected` fails on the `default_agent` check at :472 before the top-level loop). Fix: restate the sentence as "the twelve that route through `check_document`", and say why the other eight still passed.
5. [MINOR] tests/unreviewed_work_ack_test.rs:265 — this round repointed the `MEASURED` list at `2f7e437`, a second hard-coded SHA list with the same treadmill as `ACKNOWLEDGED`, and the presence assert at :556-563 requires that SHA to appear in `git log HEAD`. The pipeline rebuilds the delivery commit on every round on the same parent (FINDINGS.md:1102-1106), so `2f7e437` leaves HEAD's ancestry the moment the next round lands — the next round will pay two lines, not one. The phase's diagnosis names only one list and neither of its two proposed fixes covers this one: FINDINGS.md:1124-1128 ("the commit that will carry this delivery's work does not exist yet … the cost of insisting is one line per round") and the fix it offers (a commit-time dispatcher line, or a subject shape the selector misses) cannot repair a list that asserts the SHA's *presence* in the log. REPORT.md:514-521 lists only the acknowledgement entry under Known gaps. Fix: state the second list in Known gaps, or drop the delivery commit from `MEASURED` (the list's stated purpose is "the commits measured on a complete history", which the delivery commit is not).

REVIEW VERDICT: FINDINGS 5
